<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haile Selassie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haile Selassie was the last Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, who died in 1975. He went into exile after the Italian invasion of 1935 and was restored in 1941 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a>'s East African holdings collapsed. He pursued modernising reforms and became a prominent international figure, but was deposed in 1974, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haile-selassie.jpg" alt="Haile Selassie" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown; according to [1] and [2] an official portrait of which b/w copies were distributed by the Ethiopian government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haile_Selassie_in_full_dress_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Haile Selassie was born Tafari Makonnen on 23 July 1892 in the village of Ejersa Goro in the province of Hararghe. His father was Ras Makonnen Wolde Mikael, and his mother, Yeshimebet Ali Abba Jifar, was of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> and Silte descent. His royal line descended through his paternal grandmother from the Shewan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie</a>. He became titular governor of Selale in 1906 and was appointed governor of part of Sidamo in 1907. On 3 August 1911 he married Menen Asfaw, and the marriage lasted fifty years.</p><p>He rose to power as regent of Empress Zewditu from 1916, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-v">Lij Iyasu</a>, the designated but uncrowned emperor, was deposed on 27 September 1916. In 1924 he toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, chiefly hoping to win <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> access to the sea, which the French Foreign Ministry told him would not be granted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tafari became Negus in 1928 and emperor in 1930, taking the name Haile Selassie, which he had received at baptism. He introduced a constitution in 1931 and abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1942. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> invaded in 1935, partly to avenge its defeat at Adwa, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> failed to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. He went into exile in the United Kingdom, later travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, and returned after British forces, including Colonel Orde Wingate's Gideon Force, and Ethiopian resistance liberated the country.</p><p>After the war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> became a charter member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. On 2 December 1950 the General Assembly's Resolution 390 (V) ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> to Ethiopia, and the emperor later dissolved the federation and annexed it as a province. In November 1955 he introduced a revised constitution that made the lower house of parliament elected while he kept effective power. In 1963 he presided over the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> and was its first chairman. Critics, among them Human Rights Watch, called his administration autocratic, and some sources say the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> language was in practice kept out of education and administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In February 1974 riots against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> left five dead, the army mutinied, and strikes and protests followed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a>, deposed him in 1974. It was revealed in 1994 that the Derg had killed him at the Jubilee Palace on 27 August 1975. His bones were found in 1992 under a concrete slab on the palace grounds, and he was buried on 5 November 2000 at the Holy Trinity Cathedral in Addis Ababa. He belonged to the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church. Among followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari</a>, a movement that arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in the 1930s, he is viewed as a messiah, which he did not affirm.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haile Selassie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In July it won from Emperor <b>Haile Selassie</b> the power to arrest officials at every level, imprisoned both former prime ministers and most of their cabinets, and on 12 September 1974 deposed and imprisoned the emperor himself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Haile Selassie</b>'s attempts to reform land tenure after 1930 repeatedly stalled against noble resistance, and the unreformed system produced the rural poverty and elite resentment that enabled the Derg's seizure of power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In East Africa, British and Commonwealth forces launched a co-ordinated campaign in 1940–1941 that rapidly dismantled Italian rule in Ethiopia, Eritrea and Somaliland, and Emperor <b>Haile Selassie</b> re-entered Addis Ababa on 5 May 1941.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its name comes from &quot;Ras Tafari Makonnen&quot;, the pre-regnal title of <b>Haile Selassie</b>, and it is unknown why the early Rastas chose this form of his name.</blockquote><a href="/religion/rastafari">Rastafari · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The compromise was the Organisation of African Unity, whose charter thirty-two states signed in Addis Ababa on 25 May 1963 at a summit hosted by Emperor <b>Haile Selassie</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1975 about half of Africa’s states were under military rule, among them Ethiopia, where the Derg deposed Emperor <b>Haile Selassie</b> in 1974, and Nigeria, governed by officers from 1966 to 1979 and again from 1983 to 1999.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ejersa Goro, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.49,42.23&amp;year=1965" aria-label="Ejersa Goro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Haile Selassie"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6024 4192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6024 4192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.49,42.23&amp;year=1965">What was here in 1965? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
